/tmp/tmp.y7X5pe7ToP/diff.tex:1492:\subsubsection{The kernel attack.}
/tmp/tmp.y7X5pe7ToP/diff.tex:1494:This attack was described in \cite{GC00}. The idea of the attack consists \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{on }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{in }\DIFaddend sampling random vectors, \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{and }\DIFdelend hoping that they are in the kernel of $\Ev$, and \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{deduce }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{deducing }\DIFaddend a linear system of equations. Its complexity is equal to:\DIFdelbegin \begin{displaymath}\DIFdel{O(q^{r\lceil \frac{k}{m} \rceil}k^\omega)}\end{displaymath}%DIFAUXCMD
/tmp/tmp.y7X5pe7ToP/diff.tex:1501:This algebraic attack was introduced and studied in \cite{FSS10}. This modeling uses the minors of $\Ev$. This method has been improved in \cite{GND23}. We refer to these papers for the complexity of the attack.
/tmp/tmp.y7X5pe7ToP/diff.tex:1503:\subsubsection{Support Minors attack.}
